## The idea

We only need counts for the last W seconds, so keep a **ring of W slots, one per second**. The slot for second t is `t % W`. Each slot remembers **which second it currently holds**. If a new second lands on a slot that still holds an old second, we reset the slot first. Counting adds up only the slots whose second is still inside the window. Memory stays fixed at W slots however many events arrive. One lock makes it safe for many threads.

## Step by step

1. Make two arrays of length W: counts[] and seconds[] (which second each slot holds; −1 = never used).
2. Record(now, n): slot = now % W. If seconds[slot] != now, the slot holds an old second — set seconds[slot] = now and counts[slot] = 0. Then counts[slot] += n.
3. Count(now): add counts[i] for every slot where now − seconds[i] < W and seconds[i] ≤ now. An event exactly W seconds old is outside.
4. Wrap both methods in one lock, so an increment is never lost and a reader never sees a half-reset slot.

## Worked example

**Input:** W = 300; Record(1), Record(1), Record(299), Record(300)

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| Record(1) ×2 | slot 1: second 1, count 2 |
| Record(299) | slot 299: second 299, count 1 |
| Record(300) | slot 0: second 300, count 1 |
| Count(300) | ages 299, 1, 0 are all < 300 → 2 + 1 + 1 = 4 |
| Count(301) | second 1 is now 300 old → outside → 1 + 1 = 2 |
| Record(601) | slot 601 % 300 = 1 holds second 1 → reset, then count 1 |

**Result:** 4, then 2; slot 1 is reused for second 601 after being reset

## Cost

Record is O(1). Count is O(W) — 300 additions, which is tiny and does not grow with traffic. Memory is O(W). With very many threads, the single lock becomes the bottleneck; see the follow-ups.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// Counts events in the last windowSecs seconds. An event at time t counts while now - t < windowSecs.
public sealed class WindowCounter {
    private readonly object _lock = new object();
    private readonly int _window;
    private readonly long[] _counts;     // events recorded in that slot's second
    private readonly long[] _seconds;    // which second the slot currently holds (-1 = unused)

    public WindowCounter(int windowSecs = 300) {
        if (windowSecs <= 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(windowSecs));
        _window = windowSecs;
        _counts = new long[windowSecs];
        _seconds = new long[windowSecs];
        for (int i = 0; i < windowSecs; i++) _seconds[i] = -1;
    }

    public void Record(long nowSecs, int n = 1) {
        int slot = (int)(nowSecs % _window);
        lock (_lock) {
            if (_seconds[slot] != nowSecs) {
                // The slot still holds an older second: it has left the window, so start fresh.
                _seconds[slot] = nowSecs;
                _counts[slot] = 0;
            }
            _counts[slot] += n;
        }
    }

    public long Count(long nowSecs) {
        long total = 0;
        lock (_lock) {
            for (int i = 0; i < _window; i++) {
                long age = nowSecs - _seconds[i];
                // Inside the window: not in the future, and younger than the window.
                if (_seconds[i] >= 0 && age >= 0 && age < _window) total += _counts[i];
            }
        }
        return total;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Window edge — is an event exactly W seconds old counted?**

No: (now − W, now]

**Q: Read cost?**

O(W); O(1) with a running total

**Q: Record from many threads.**

Stamp check-and-reset is the race → lock

**Q: Per user, ten million users.**

Sketches; coarser buckets

**Q: Across machines.**

Local counters, periodic aggregation

